			<div class="content" id="post-content-4815569">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Not anymore!</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Informative/Drama</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> Standard SCP article with interview and containment updates.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>Comatose old man pulls nearby sleepers into customized dream reality.</li>
<li>Emotional state of the man affects range of 'pull' (happier=less range, key to effective containment.)</li>
<li>Bodies of sleepers appear braindead; their consciousnesses return when awakened (though they will not wake without external influence like alarms.)</li>
<li>Dream reality defaults as a void but is sculpted into things like houses by the SCP.</li>
<li>Anyone traveling into the dream appears there in an incorporeal form until given a body by the SCP.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>An interview with the SCP after recovery reveals he is an emotional wreck after spending a week alone, stuck in a void (that he had not learned to control yet.)</li>
<li>After several weeks of ineffective containment, Foundation therapists entered the dream and assessed the SCP's emotional state. The SCP's wife (who had been fed a cover that he had died in the hospital) was brought into the Foundation as a last resort for cheap and effective containment of the SCP.</li>
<li>Because she is not able to sleep near him 24/7, Class C Level 2 personnel are allowed to enter the dream to socialize with the SCP while she is awake (any essential staff is not allowed in because of the uncertain nature of the reality.)</li>
<li>The central narrative covers the SCP coming to terms with its condition and its inability to return to the real world. I intend to format this in an &quot;emotional-emotionless&quot; way, where: despite being a scientific document, conveys strong elements to the reader by using their own imagination against them.</li>
<li>The article will have slow emotional progression disguised by the use of containment updates which contain &quot;range updates&quot; showing spikes and decreases with notes alongside.</li>
<li>Sometime after the SCP reaches a containable situation, his wife's body dies while asleep and her mind becomes stuck with him in the dream.</li>
<li>Sometime after the above bullet, there is a second strong interview with him where the full extent of his progression is shown to the reader.</li>
</ul>
<p>Other:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Foundation decided that bringing the SCP's wife in to handle its emotional state was a better (cheaper) option than creating a cell that was several miles away from any civilian residence to store it.</li>
<li>There are more details about the story along with recovery specifics and different endings in my critter responses.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The range of this SCP is quite alarming.
