<blockquote>
<p>Containment logs written by the researcher overlooking the SCP will cover the narrative of the SCP's progression from a distressed state to a containable situation (the interview of it will cover the SCP's coming to terms with its condition.) The penultimate of which is the death of the SCP's wife while asleep, causing her to become an instance of SCP-XXXX-2 and forever trapped within SCP-XXXX-1. Finishing with the researcher remarking that any future catastrophes are unlikely and suggesting reclassifying the SCP from Keter to Euclid.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have some questions around this:</p>
<ul>
<li>What do you mean by 'containment logs'? Is this just gonna be like, short containment procedures updates? Or longer interview style logs about the containment or?</li>
<li>How does the progression occur? My concern is that it feels very passive (i.e, researchers go into the dream more and stuff just gets better); while it really feels like it needs to be active (i.e., there should be some obstacle or difficulty, so the journey to achieve this is exciting; but also people should be actively doing stuff to cause it to happen)</li>
<li>The death of the wife here feels&#8230;sudden? Like it feels kinda emotionally like it happens at the wrong time; since it feels like it'd make a greater impetus for the original depression than a generic containment thing; but also it happens right when stuff is mostly better emotionally for the SCP, so it feels like it should be a setback but then&#8230;nothing really happens as a result</li>
</ul>
