<p>1. I should have specified, they are short containment updates, I didn't want longer ones because of the two interviews that go along with them. They will be not unlike the ones in <a href="/scp-1730">SCP-1730</a>, though drastically less violent.</p>
<p>2. The researchers would be suggesting and implementing prescriptions to little avail until someone suggests bringing Susan in. Following that, foundation personnel who are architects, historians, and such will have noted jumps in lowering the range as they help the SCP live in a world that feels real.</p>
<p>The obstacle in the way is the SCP's slipping grasp on what everything is supposed to look like in detail, like how you might not remember the face of a friend who died years ago without looking at a photograph, something the SCP doesn't have access to. Grass won't be the right shade of green, food won't taste the same (and he doesn't need to eat it anyway.) The foundation personnel aid him in creating objects that are &quot;right&quot; along with traveling through the world with him. Successful instances of object creation will have marked decreases in effect range.</p>
<p>3. There would be some jump in the dates on the logs between the SCP becoming very manageable and the death of its wife. The SCP and its wife would both be pretty old so I reasoned she would knock off sooner rather than later later. I didn't have room for a full description of SCP-XXXX-2 above, but it means that her consciousness was inside SCP-XXXX-1 at the time of death, causing her to be trapped in the dream with the SCP, and the only reason Susan was onsite in the first place was to keep the SCP happy, I figured that her forever being with him would be an improvement on the situation.</p>
