<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit&nbsp;response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I oversimplified to shorten the pitch up top, but it is a mix of both.</p>
<hr />
<p>The core narrative is simply this man's struggle to cope with his new (literally) reality and the Foundation's efforts to make him happy with amenities like his wife and personnel access (along with restrictions to what personnel can and cannot do with him.) It starts dark with an MTF's report on their recovery operation and the interview with the SCP where he is revealed to pretty be much a wreck. And then it slowly becomes heartwarming with the containment updates showing his progress and increasing ability to stay happy and be with his wife. It ends as a mix of both with the death of his wife while asleep, causing her to become stuck within SCP-XXXX-1 forever, this comes along with a second interview sometime after where he is nice and happy with his wife and there are some bad things but it's alright. Then it finishes with an email from the lead researcher of SCP-XXXX to the site assistant head of containment with a request for a downgrade in containment class because of the emotional progression (Keter to Euclid.)</p>
<p>If I went with all dark or all heartwarming I was worried there would be no feel of progression through the story. When written, all of the skip's personal progress is disguised as the Foundation simply wanting better containment.</p>
<hr />
<p>I was also toying with the idea that at the bottom there would be a link to an &quot;updated page&quot; where it is revealed that the SCP passed away sometime after the second interview (a year or so after) and the Foundation has no way of accessing the dream and no way of knowing whether he and his wife are still away in his reality or not.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;Do you want to possibly explore why he ended up this way perhaps?&quot;</p>
<p>I was thinking about having his wife state something about how she always had weird dreams when her husband was very upset but that she never thought anything of it because they were just dreams. My current explanation is that he was just born with the ability to dream realities and was unaware of it for most of his life until it was kicked into overdrive when he became comatose.</p>
</div>
