<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">[Placeholder, will be looking over it by tomorrow.]</span></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;Review</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Fold&nbsp;collapsible</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Could you please update your pitch next time so that it is easier for reviewers to stay up-to-date with the current changes you have in mind? From your pitch itself, I couldn't see much narrative (you just described the threat it poses) or connection to a theme so I read your reply to Cyvsti's comment.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The core narrative is simply this man's struggle to cope with his new (literally) reality</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So why did these effects manifest in the first place? You mentioned he was a wreck but is this new reality he creates a coping mechanism or a mechanism he has to cope with?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foundation's efforts to make him happy with amenities like his wife and personnel access (along with restrictions to what personnel can and cannot do with him.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why does Foundation want to make him happy? I mean, if personnel that enter this state where they are put into that reality but can still be woken up with alarms or other relatively normal effects, they can just continue studying him easily? Why does the Foundation want to make him happy? They're not a hotel or anything, they're a scientific research facility.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And then it slowly becomes heartwarming with the containment updates showing his progress and increasing ability to stay happy and be with his wife.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The problem with this is that no conflict was introduced in the beginning. If the story is just his progress to getting happier with his wife, then there needs to be something that made them unhappy in the first place, right? I also find it infeasible that the Foundation would allow the wife to tend to the man. Firstly, it's impractical to reveal to a civilian that this hidden world of monsters and anomalies exist when Foundation isn't even sure that improving the relationship between the old man and the wife can stop these effects (which in the first place, don't really need too many expensive measures to counter).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It ends as a mix of both with the death of his wife while asleep</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See? Foundation could have easily avoided this casualty (or at least, wouldn't seem like they are responsible for it) if they just amnesticised the wife and let her live her life out. And why this ending? It just seems so contrived to deliver a gut-punch artificially that it seems like a lazy and inadequate ending. There are still questions left unanswered like: &quot;Why would the Foundation commit such an impractical folly?&quot;, &quot;Why did the effects manifest in the first place?&quot;, &quot;What even is the significance of the reality being of old Britain?&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Then it finishes with an email from the lead researcher of SCP-XXXX to the site assistant head of containment with a request for a downgrade in containment class because of the emotional progression</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It was a Euclid in the first place, according to the effects. The class change is also kinda cheesy, but that's just my opinion because I've seen quite a few unjustified/unsatisfyingly-explained class changes.</p>
<p>Right now, the explanation for the reason of manifestation of these abilities just don't connect thematically either. This idea needs a little bit more work, because although I am not happy with some unexplained stuff, the story is quaint and tragic and it could be a good work if done right.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

