<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I have explaining to do then&#8230;</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;Could you please update your pitch next time?&quot;</p>
<p>With all of the stuff I included in the responses, I would be well over the 400 limit. I actually had to remove narrative stuff to include the pointer to my response to Cyvstvi. Sorry about that.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;So why did these effects manifest in the first place?&quot;</p>
<p>As stated in my response to Cyvstvi, there really wasn't a reason, he was just born with it and the power was kicked into overdrive when he went comatose. Also, he became a wreck after being stuck in isolation for a week in this dark void and away from his wife (and he had yet to realize his power there) not because of some beforehand incident.</p>
<p>I toyed with the idea that his parents were some occult practitioners, but it seemed too cheesy to be the answer.</p>
<p>Also I thought that perhaps he couldn't remember having any parents and that he came from an orphanage and didn't know the specifics of his birth.</p>
<p>Since I was undecided, I went with a &quot;no reason for it&quot; approach.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;There needs to be something that made them unhappy in the first place&quot;<br />
And<br />
&quot;The problem with this is that no conflict was introduced in the beginning.&quot;</p>
<p>A week of complete isolation and never being able to go back to the real world causing his effect range to be several miles in diameter.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;I also find it infeasible that the Foundation would allow the wife to tend to the man.&quot;</p>
<p>She tends to him so the Foundation can cut containment cost, and without her he would never be adequately contained.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;It was a Euclid in the first place.&quot;</p>
<p>It was deemed Keter after it was able to affect most of a city's sleeping inhabitants. It is able to affect anything from several miles away if not dealt with properly. And the class change is to reinforce the idea that the old man has really coped and is happy now.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;Why does the Foundation want to make him happy?&quot;<br />
conflicts with:<br />
&quot;Allowing the anomaly to enter a depressive state may have disastrous consequences relating to its mood-range relation (a good mood reduces the range of the SCP drastically) given the specifics of its capture (a very large area around and including a hospital affected by the SCP.)&quot;</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;(Which in the first place, don't really need too many expensive measures to counter).&quot;</p>
<p>I'd love to hear your thoughts on that, but the wife <em>is</em> the inexpensive measure, a single person able to negate the SCP's effects when exposed. And she is not allowed to leave the site, she lives there and sleeps whenever practical.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;What even is the significance of the reality being of old Britain?&quot;</p>
<p>That's pretty much just fluff designed to tell you exactly what I mean when I say &quot;the reality can easily be manipulated into taking shape by the SCP&quot; and will probably be removed in the draft.</p>
<hr />
<hr />
<p>Perhaps there's too many words in all of the post to be totally coherent to any random reader, but I feel like you missed some key things that were definitely in there. I believe I addressed all of your critiques there, except for why the effects manifested, the reasons for that are still a work in progress.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>After an extensive IRC conversation, I have rewritten much of the original post.</p>

							</div>

								
