<p>I got summoned by IRC Chat. Let's crit this.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Here,&nbsp;have&nbsp;some&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;OK,&nbsp;now&nbsp;give&nbsp;me&nbsp;back&nbsp;my&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">A comatose old man &quot;traps&quot; any nearby (range fluctuates based on mood) sleeper's consciousness in a separate reality</span></p>
</blockquote>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Is this voluntary or you get trapped while just walking by? I would be really annoying for both the person that gets trapped and the old man who now has an unwanted visitor.</span></p>
<p>My reading comprehension is failing. Sorry.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;for all we know, that thing reads the mind of anyone in it and implants memories.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not entirely sure about this. It would still be a gigantic security risk. I think the Foundation would manage to keep the man in a good mood with the help of his wife. Maybe they could try including close friends or relatives? Or maybe just Level 1 personnel with written permission?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It ends as a mix of both with the death of his wife while asleep, causing her to become stuck within SCP-XXXX-1 forever</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I actually was thinking about this ending while reading the concept. Yeah, this would be a good ending.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it is revealed that the SCP passed away sometime after the second interview (a year or so after) and the Foundation has no way of accessing the dream and no way of knowing whether he and his wife are still away in his reality or not.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This ending can work, but I think you should write the man's perspective about death in an interview or something, so it gets more emotional impact on the reader. And also include the perspective of his wife, as she seems to be a key character to the story.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, I think this has potential to make people cry.</p>
<p>Feel free to contact a member of the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster">Butterfly Squad</a> to get more crit, or PM me to take another look.</p>

