<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit&nbsp;response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<hr />
<p>&quot;I found the initial Elevator Pitch very bland&quot;</p>
<p>I suppose that's why we do narrative driven SCP's these days.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;You can easily headcanon an Onerei-trained researcher with some device that lets you record dream brainwaves and convert them into videos, but then what? A series of emotional interviews, I feel like would be inadequate.&quot;</p>
<p>Have you ever read <a href="/scp-990">SCP-990</a>? Where the researcher writes down the interview after it happened, it'll go something like that.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;Stuffing him in a containment cell away from 'nearby' civilians and just putting him on life support.&quot;</p>
<p>There's a sentence in the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/how-to-write-an-scp">How to Write an SCP Guide</a> that states &quot;the Foundation is cold, not cruel; yes, we could stick anomalous fellow human beings in an empty concrete cell without windows, but that will only get us terminally depressed SCPs.&quot;</p>
<p>I'm using that as headcanon to say that the Foundation wouldn't do something like that.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;Shoehorning personnel in there for interviews would be way too forced and awkward.&quot;</p>
<p>Considering the fact that it is a scientific document, I would feel they were not completely out of place. But I will keep a sharp eye on it to make sure it isn't.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;The most realistic thing they'd end up doing is amnesticizing her.&quot;</p>
<p>In my post I briefly touched on the fact that she was led (by the Foundation) to believe that &quot;her husband died in the hospital.&quot; Me and forbiddenquest were talking on IRC about how perhaps they told her he had died and there was a mix-up at the morgue and his body was incinerated because (headcanon alert) they couldn't just amnesticize half her life away.</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;Why would they just include some civilian woman in their operations? The Foundation doesn't care about emotions.&quot;</p>
<p>In this case, emotions directly correspond to how dangerous and effective the SCP is, I state that they &quot;brought [her] in as a last resort&quot; and that &quot;several Foundation therapists communed with the SCP and concluded that the only way to keep a consistent mood would be to bring in its wife.&quot;</p>
<hr />
<p>&quot;I just can't think of a way you could make this work via an emotionless post-mortem scientific document.&quot;</p>
<p>The format I am shooting for is an emotional emotionless, which is an oxymoron but I mean to convey strong emotion through the lack of it in the form of containment updates and the interviews (which will be rich in one-sided feeling.) The updates will disguise his emotional progression with range decreases along with emotionless notes. I'm trying to use the reader's own imagination to convey strong emotion through an emotionless document concerning the story of two lovers trapped in their reality and unable to return. Kind of how <a href="/scp-087">SCP-087</a> creates fear out of some face following you around, but instead of fear its a love story and stuff.</p>
<hr />
<p>I do apologize for the mess, maybe I'll rewrite it again. If I could get to the draft stage already I wouldn't be over encumbered with so much detail. Thank you for your crit.</p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>

