			<div class="content" id="post-content-4821854">
									

<p>Hello, again.</p>
<p>I'm back mainly to touch up on the poorly touched parts that I touched earlier. I'm usually more organized than this, but I had a pretty difficult time just picking through this concept page.</p>
<p>Let me address a few things.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>1.) Elevator pitch: Yeah my bad, I meant to follow up that even the blandest <strong>Elevator Pitch</strong> can be supplemented with a good enough plot.</p>
<p>2.) No, I never read scp-990. I'll do that soon.</p>
<p>3.) &quot;Stuffing him in a containment cell away from 'nearby' civilians and just putting him on life support.&quot;</p>
<p>Cold, not cruel, I get it. What I meant was that they'd stick him in a cell and do some regular checkups mentally but really not much else. Apologies I was unclear there.</p>
<p>4.) &quot;Shoehorning personnel in there for interviews would be way too forced and awkward.&quot;</p>
<p>What I meant by this is that these intimate, intense and emotional moments should be reserved for just this couple and this couple alone&#8230;putting personnel in there would, imo, just feel weird.</p>
<p>5.) In my post I briefly touched on the fact that she was led (by the Foundation) to believe that &quot;her husband died in the hospital.&quot; Me and forbiddenquest were talking on IRC about how perhaps they told her he had died and there was a mix-up at the morgue and his body was incinerated because (headcanon alert) they couldn't just amnesticize half her life away.</p>
<p>My apologies for missing that in the post. Just sloppiness on my behalf. That headcanon does make sense.</p>
<p>6.) The format I am shooting for is an emotional emotionless, which is an oxymoron but I mean to convey strong emotion through the lack of it in the form of containment updates and the interviews (which will be rich in one-sided feeling.) The updates will disguise his emotional progression with range decreases along with emotionless notes. I'm trying to use the reader's own imagination to convey strong emotion through an emotionless document concerning the story of two lovers trapped in their reality and unable to return. Kind of how SCP-087 creates fear out of some face following you around, but instead of fear its a love story and stuff.</p>
<p>Okay. I'm liking this.</p>
<p>Okay, I apologise again on some of my earlier sloppiness. I'm starting to like where this is going. Especially with number 6 on my list.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I do apologize for the mess, maybe I'll rewrite it again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Actually, I do know of a way you can super-condense information while staying below the 400 word limit.</p>
<ul>
<li>Bullet points!</li>
<li>trust me</li>
<li>This saves space.</li>
</ul>
<p>Really, I'm starting to get a bigger picture of what you're trying to portray, and I'm getting more on board with this.</p>
<p>Just rewrite the concept page to be less messy and more accurately show everything you want to. It's possible, trust me.</p>
<p>Like, for example:</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Comatose man pulls nearby sleepers into customized dream reality.</li>
<li>Emotional states affects range of 'pull.'</li>
<li>Bodies of sleepers appear braindead; their consciousnesses return when awakened.</li>
</ul>
<p>See? I just condensed your large Elevator Pitch into three sentences. The blank white void thing as well as the intangible body things are both tangential and can be just brought up in the draft later.</p>
<p>Proper condensing of information is possible.</p>
<p>You got this.</p>
<p>Best of luck.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

