<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;"><em>PM request placeholder</em></span></p>
<p>Alrighty then, let's take another look, shall we?</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The article will have slow emotional progression disguised by the use of containment updates which contain &quot;range updates&quot; showing spikes and decreases with notes alongside.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Really, this is the part that impresses me most. Using a bland, emotionless scientific document to portray deeply emotional occurrences is very clever to me and the moment you stated this, I was more intrigued.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sometime after the SCP reaches a containable situation, his wife's body dies while asleep and her mind becomes stuck with him in the dream.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm struggling to think of how you could portray this because this seems a little too&#8230;convenient?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sometime after the above bullet, there is a second strong interview with him where the full extent of his progression is shown to the reader.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hmmmmmm I have a funny idea that I'm not sure if you'll be on board with or not.</p>
<p>Maybe have one personnel offer their own takes on it whenever they write down a note. Or speak to their director or some such. Having a Foundation personnel get emotional from witnessing the emotional scene. It would be&#8230;weird.</p>
<p>We, the readers, aren't witnessing ANYTHING in the dreams. We're reading as a third perspective while the document shows personnel from a secondary perspective.</p>
<p>So, like, we're witnessing an empathetic researcher get emotional over an emotional scene we're not allowed to directly witness to show just how disconnected this SCP is.</p>
<p>You see what I'm getting at?</p>
<p>I feel like you could find a way to loop the researcher's observations into the Containment Procedures and Description in a way where it all clicks and makes sense in the end and makes it super emotional.</p>
<p>Apologies if this point in particular is a little confusing or if I'm being unclear.</p>
<p>Either way, you seem to have a solid grasp on what story you want to tell. You're passionate, persistent and adamant about wanting to tell this story.</p>
<p>So. Here's your <strong><em><span style="color: green">GREENLIGHT</span></em></strong>.</p>
<p>I'm still kinda worried about how this will be presented in the end, but that's what critique and drafting are for.</p>
<p>Feel free to PM me for draft crit if you wish.</p>
<p>Good luck!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

